Thought for 11s
Yep — this is the classic Swing text-document gremlin.

Your problem is almost certainly this line:

int blockEnd = endElem.getEndOffset();
On the last line of a JTextComponent document, Element.getEndOffset() is often doc.getLength() + 1, not doc.getLength(). Swing does this because its line model is a tiny bit cursed and likes to pretend there is always one more position at the end.

So when you do:

int blockLen = blockEnd - blockStart;
String blockText = doc.getText(blockStart, blockLen);
you can end up asking for text past the actual document length, which throws BadLocationException.

Fix
Clamp the end offset:

int docLen = doc.getLength();

int blockStart = startElem.getStartOffset();
int blockEnd = Math.min(endElem.getEndOffset(), docLen);
int blockLen = blockEnd - blockStart;
That is the big one.

You also have a boundary bug here
This check:

if ((dir < 0 && startLine == 0) || (dir > 0 && endLine >= root.getElementCount())) {
    return;
}
should be:

if ((dir < 0 && startLine == 0) || (dir > 0 && endLine >= root.getElementCount() - 1)) {
    return;
}
Because the last valid line index is:

root.getElementCount() - 1
Not root.getElementCount().

