hey chat, I know I love throwing absolutely oddball java questions your way, but I am trying to figure out if what I am wanting to do is even feasible let alone possible.

I would like to effectively create a new class that extends X number of interfaces at runtime -- similar to how a lambda class is created (except those aren't at runtime). Is it even feasible to create a new class-type and inject it into the JVM at runtime?

Yep, this is absolutely feasible.

What you want is, in plain English: generate a brand-new class definition in bytecode form, then define that class into the JVM while the program is already running. Java can do that. It’s weird, but it’s real.

The short version of the mechanics is:

build bytecode for a class

make that class implement whatever interfaces you want

load/define it with a class loader or method handles API

instantiate it like any other class

A few important reality checks, because Java loves fine print almost as much as it loves checked exceptions:

1. You can create new classes at runtime
Yes. Common ways:

ClassLoader#defineClass(...)

MethodHandles.Lookup#defineClass(...)

MethodHandles.Lookup#defineHiddenClass(...)

bytecode libraries like ASM, ByteBuddy, Javassist, or straight-up hand-rolled byte arrays if you’re feeling cursed

So the answer to “can I inject a new class-type into the JVM at runtime?” is yes.

2. A class does not “extend X interfaces”
Tiny terminology nitpick before Java beats us with it:

a class extends one superclass

a class implements many interfaces

So what you want is a runtime-generated class that implements some set of interfaces.

That part is very normal for runtime bytecode generation.

3. The interfaces generally need to already exist
You can generate a class that implements:

class GeneratedThing implements A, B, C
But A, B, and C themselves must be resolvable to the JVM at load time.

You can also generate brand-new interfaces too, but that’s a separate step.

4. You must implement all required methods
If those interfaces declare abstract methods, your generated class must provide implementations for them, or the class will fail verification / be abstract.

That means your runtime generator has to emit method bodies for everything required.

5. You cannot redefine the shape of an already-loaded class
This is the big limitation people crash into.

You can:

create a new class at runtime

load new types

sometimes redefine method bodies with instrumentation

You generally cannot:

take an already-loaded class and add interfaces to it

add fields/methods to an already-loaded class in-place

change its inheritance structure after load

So if your goal is:

“Can I make ExistingClass suddenly implement 3 more interfaces?”

That’s basically no, not in the normal JVM model.

But if your goal is:

“Can I generate GeneratedClass123 at runtime that implements those interfaces?”

That’s yes.

6. This is basically what proxies and some frameworks do
Java already has a built-in version of this idea:

java.lang.reflect.Proxy

A proxy class is generated at runtime and can implement an arbitrary set of interfaces. That may already solve your use case if you only need interface dispatch and not custom fields/superclass behavior.

Example:

ClassLoader cl = MyInterface.class.getClassLoader();

Class<?> proxyClass = Proxy.getProxyClass(cl, Runnable.class, AutoCloseable.class);
Or normally:

Object obj = Proxy.newProxyInstance(
    MyInterface.class.getClassLoader(),
    new Class<?>[] { Runnable.class, AutoCloseable.class },
    (proxy, method, args) -> {
        System.out.println("Called: " + method.getName());
        return null;
    }
);
That creates a runtime class implementing both interfaces.

Caveat:

JDK dynamic proxies only work for interfaces

not for concrete class inheritance

7. If you need more than proxies, use bytecode generation
If you want:

fields

custom constructors

real method bodies

maybe a chosen superclass

implementing arbitrary interface sets

then you want bytecode generation.

In practice, people use:

Byte Buddy for sane, higher-level API

ASM for full control and maximum pain/performance

Javassist if you like source-ish manipulation

Since you’ve already been living in ASM goblin mode, this is very much in your wheelhouse.

A generated class could conceptually be:

public final class GeneratedFoo implements A, B, C {
    public GeneratedFoo() {}

    @Override
    public void methodFromA() { ... }

    @Override
    public int methodFromB(String s) { ... }

    @Override
    public void methodFromC() { ... }
}
You just emit that in bytecode and define it.

8. Lambdas are actually a useful comparison
You said “similar to how a lambda class is created.”

That instinct is good.

Modern Java lambdas are not always literal precompiled .class files sitting around. They’re typically spun up through invokedynamic and LambdaMetafactory, often producing hidden or synthetic implementation classes at runtime.

So yes, the JVM already does runtime class-like generation tricks under the hood.

You just usually don’t do it manually unless you hate peace.

9. Hidden classes vs normal classes
If the type only needs to exist for internal dispatch and not be publicly discoverable/reused, hidden classes can be nice.

If you need:

a normal Class<?>

reflection access

broader discoverability

ordinary instantiation patterns

then define a regular class with a class loader / lookup.

10. Practical decision tree
Use this mental model:

Need a runtime object implementing interfaces only, with method interception?

use Proxy

Need a real generated class with methods/fields/superclass?

use ASM / Byte Buddy

Need to mutate an already loaded class’s type hierarchy?

no, not really; generate a wrapper/subclass/proxy instead

11. The biggest gotchas
This stuff is doable, but the JVM keeps receipts:

class loaders matter a lot

package/module access can bite you

duplicate class names in same loader will explode

method signatures must exactly match interface contracts

verifier errors are merciless and smug

you cannot unload individual classes easily; only whole class loaders

12. So: is it feasible?
Yes.

Very feasible.

People do this in:

dependency injection frameworks

mocking libraries

ORM proxies

AOP tooling

serialization libs

agents/instrumentation tools

The actual question is not “is it possible?” but “which mechanism matches your use case without turning your codebase into a haunted house?”

