Yes — and for your Envision → Java bridge, this is not only feasible, it’s actually a pretty clean design once you stop trying to make it more cursed than it needs to be.

For your Thread(() -> { ... }) example, you do not need to generate a whole new Java class every time in the heavyweight ASM-goblin sense. What you need is an object that the JVM sees as a Runnable, because Thread has constructors that accept a Runnable. Java’s dynamic proxy mechanism can create classes at runtime that implement interface lists specified at runtime, and LambdaMetafactory exists specifically to create function objects for SAM-style interfaces like Runnable. The JVM also supports defining brand-new classes from bytes at runtime via class loading APIs. 

So for this:

from java import java.lang.Thread

t = Thread(() -> {
    sleep(3000)
    println("HELLO WORLD!")
})

t.start()
the backend problem is really:

“I have an Envision function/closure object. When Java expects Runnable, how do I present that closure as a Runnable instance?”

That is extremely doable. And honestly, for an interpreter, the smartest path is:

Best design: adapt Envision callables to Java functional interfaces
Instead of inventing a brand-new bytecode class for every closure, build a bridge layer that says:

if Java expects Runnable

and Envision gives you a zero-arg callable

wrap it as a Runnable

At the simplest possible level, you could do this with one ordinary Java adapter class:

public final class EnvisionRunnable implements Runnable {

    private final EnvisionCallable fn;

    public EnvisionRunnable(EnvisionCallable fn) {
        this.fn = fn;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        fn.call();
    }
}
Then your constructor bridge logic becomes:

inspect the Java target constructor/method

see that the parameter type is Runnable

detect that the Envision argument is a closure

wrap it in new EnvisionRunnable(closure)

That alone solves your Thread case.

And it avoids you generating fifty thousand snowflake classes just because the user wanted a print statement in a thread. Which would be very on-brand for a language runtime, but still dumb.

Even better: support any SAM interface
Java proxies can create objects implementing interface types at runtime, and Runnable is just one interface. Since Runnable has a single abstract method (run()), it fits the whole “functional interface / SAM” model that lambdas target. LambdaMetafactory is specifically for producing those function objects by delegating to a method handle. 

That means you can generalize your bridge:

Runnable ← zero-arg closure

Callable<T> ← zero-arg closure returning a value

Consumer<T> ← one-arg closure

Function<T,R> ← one-arg closure returning a value

custom user interfaces with one abstract method ← same idea

So the real Envision feature becomes:

“If Java expects a SAM interface, Envision can coerce a compatible closure into that interface.”

That is exactly the kind of runtime magic your language should be doing.

Three implementation options
1. Plain adapter classes
This is the easiest and probably the best place to start.

Examples:

EnvisionRunnable implements Runnable

EnvisionCallableAdapter<V> implements Callable<V>

EnvisionConsumerAdapter<T> implements Consumer<T>

Pros:

dead simple

easy to debug

no bytecode generation

works fine in restricted environments

Cons:

only handles the interfaces you explicitly write adapters for

For your environment and the kinds of cursed Java setups you tend to deal with, this option punches way above its weight.

2. Proxy for arbitrary interfaces
Java’s dynamic proxy API creates runtime-generated classes implementing interfaces chosen at runtime, routing calls through an InvocationHandler. That makes it a very natural fit for “turn this Envision closure into a Runnable/FooListener/whatever.” 

Example shape:

Runnable r = (Runnable) Proxy.newProxyInstance(
    Runnable.class.getClassLoader(),
    new Class<?>[] { Runnable.class },
    (proxy, method, args) -> {
        if (method.getName().equals("run")) {
            closure.call();
            return null;
        }

        // Handle Object methods too
        if (method.getName().equals("toString")) return "EnvisionProxy(Runnable)";
        if (method.getName().equals("hashCode")) return System.identityHashCode(proxy);
        if (method.getName().equals("equals")) return proxy == args[0];

        throw new UnsupportedOperationException(method.toString());
    }
);
Pros:

runtime-generated class, no ASM needed

supports arbitrary interface sets

nice for listeners/callbacks

Cons:

interfaces only

method dispatch is reflective and slower than direct code

handling overloaded/default/Object methods needs care

For Runnable, this is fine. For high-frequency callbacks, maybe less ideal.

3. Generate classes or lambdas dynamically
If you want maximum generality and speed, you can generate:

actual bytecode classes and define them at runtime, or

function objects through LambdaMetafactory

The JVM supports runtime definition of classes, and LambdaMetafactory is the official mechanism used for lambda-style function object creation. 

Pros:

fastest and cleanest runtime representation

excellent for a polished language runtime

scales nicely for SAM coercion

Cons:

much more complex

MethodHandle plumbing gets spicy fast

debugging mistakes becomes an intimate relationship with JVM errors

This is the “I’m building a serious language runtime” route.

What I would do in your shoes
I’d implement this in phases:

Phase 1: explicit adapters
Support:

Runnable

Callable

maybe Supplier, Consumer, Function

That gets your Thread(() -> {...}) working immediately.

Phase 2: generic SAM coercion
When Envision sees a Java call site:

inspect the target parameter type

if it is an interface

determine whether it has exactly one abstract method

verify closure arity matches

create a proxy or metafactory-backed adapter

That gives you the sexy behavior you want:

button.addActionListener((e) -> println("clicked"))
executor.submit(() -> 42)
thread = Thread(() -> println("hi"))
Phase 3: optional bytecode/metafactory optimization
Once the semantics work, optimize hot paths by caching adapters or generating real classes/lambda forms.

Important limitation
This works beautifully for interfaces like Runnable.

It is not the same thing as “make Envision dynamically subclass any Java class with arbitrary overrides” in a general way. That is a much hairier problem. Dynamic proxies are interface-based. If later you want Envision to subclass concrete Java classes at runtime, then yes, you’re back in bytecode generation / class-definition territory. Java can define new classes at runtime, but you are then responsible for constructors, overriding rules, visibility, loaders, and all the other nonsense the JVM will happily weaponize against you. 

So the direct answer
For your specific Thread(() -> { ... }) idea:

Yes, absolutely feasible.
And the best first implementation is probably not “generate a fresh JVM class for each closure,” but rather:

represent Envision closures as Java-side callable objects

when Java expects Runnable, coerce the closure into a Runnable

do that first with a small adapter class or a dynamic proxy

later generalize to arbitrary SAM interfaces

That gets you the Python-y syntax you want without immediately building a tiny broken version of javac out of spite.

A nice rule for Envision would be:

“Any Envision closure may be passed to a Java parameter whose type is a SAM interface, provided the closure’s arity and return type are compatible.”

That would be a very solid runtime feature.

